   38	
   39	## Definition
   40	
   41	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
   42	
   43	**A point outside $X$, named rather than assumed.** Put
   44	
   45	$$\infty \;:=\; \{\, y \in X : y \notin y \,\},$$
   46	
   47	a set by Separation. Then $\infty \notin X$: were $\infty \in X$, the defining
   48	condition applied to $\infty$ itself would give
   49	$\infty \in \infty \iff \infty \notin \infty$. So no hypothesis about $X$ is
   50	needed to obtain a point outside it, and the construction below is available for
   51	every space.
   52	
   53	**The space.** Put $X^{*} := X \cup \{\infty\}$ and
   54	
   55	$$\mathcal{T}^{*} \;:=\; \mathcal{T} \;\cup\; \{\, X^{*} \setminus C \;:\; C \subseteq X,\ C \text{ closed in } X \text{ and a compact subset of } X \,\} .$$
   56	
   57	The pair $(X^{*}, \mathcal{T}^{*})$ is the **one-point compactification**, or
   58	**Alexandroff compactification**, of $X$. Members of $\mathcal{T}$ are said to be
   59	of the **first kind** and the sets $X^{*} \setminus C$ of the **second kind**; a
   60	set of the second kind is exactly an open set of $\mathcal{T}^{*}$ containing
   61	$\infty$, since a member of $\mathcal{T}$ is a subset of $X$, and the set $C$ is
   62	recovered from it as $C = X^{*} \setminus (X^{*} \setminus C)$.
   63	
   64	**$\mathcal{T}^{*}$ is a topology on $X^{*}$, and this is discharged here.**
   65	Throughout, "closed" and "compact" without qualification mean closed in $X$ and a
   66	compact subset of $X$ ([[def-compact-space]]); two facts about such sets are used
   67	and both are [[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]]: a subset of a
   68	compact $C$ that is closed in $X$ is closed in the subspace $C$
   69	([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) and hence compact, and a union of two compact
   70	subsets is compact.
   71	
   72	*(T1).* $\varnothing \in \mathcal{T}$, and $X^{*} = X^{*} \setminus \varnothing$
   73	is of the second kind, $\varnothing$ being closed in $X$ and compact.
   74	
   75	*(T2).* Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}^{*}$, let $\mathcal{S}_1$ be the
   76	members of $\mathcal{S}$ lying in $\mathcal{T}$ and $\mathcal{S}_2$ the rest, so
   77	that every member of $\mathcal{S}_2$ is of the second kind. If
   78	$\mathcal{S}_2 = \varnothing$ then $\bigcup \mathcal{S} = \bigcup \mathcal{S}_1$
   79	lies in $\mathcal{T}$ by (T2) in $X$. Otherwise put
   80	$U := \bigcup \mathcal{S}_1 \in \mathcal{T}$ and
   81	$\mathcal{D} := \{\, X^{*} \setminus O : O \in \mathcal{S}_2 \,\}$, a nonempty
   82	family of closed compact subsets of $X$, and $C_0 := \bigcap \mathcal{D}$. Then
   83	$C_0$ is closed by (C2) of [[def-topological-space]], and it is a closed subset of
   84	any one member of $\mathcal{D}$, hence compact. Now
   85	
   86	$$\bigcup \mathcal{S} \;=\; U \cup (X^{*} \setminus C_0) \;=\; X^{*} \setminus (C_0 \setminus U),$$
   87	
   88	and $C_0 \setminus U = C_0 \cap (X \setminus U)$ is closed in $X$ and a subset of
   89	the compact $C_0$, hence compact; so $\bigcup \mathcal{S}$ is of the second kind.
   90	
   91	*(T3).* For $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ the intersection lies in $\mathcal{T}$ by (T3)
   92	in $X$. For two sets of the second kind,
   93	$(X^{*} \setminus C) \cap (X^{*} \setminus D) = X^{*} \setminus (C \cup D)$, and
   94	$C \cup D$ is closed in $X$ and compact as a union of two compact subsets. For one
   95	of each, $\infty \notin U$ gives
   96	$U \cap (X^{*} \setminus C) = U \cap (X \setminus C)$, an intersection of two
   97	members of $\mathcal{T}$.
   98	
   99	**Why the compact sets are also required to be closed.** The complement of a
  100	compact set that is not closed in $X$ would not make $\infty$'s neighbourhoods
  101	behave: the union computation in (T2) uses that an intersection of the discarded
  102	sets is again closed, and the intersection of arbitrary compact subsets of a
  103	non-Hausdorff space need not be compact. When $X$ is Hausdorff every compact
  104	subset is closed ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]]) and the
  105	two descriptions agree, which is why many texts state the definition without the
  106	word "closed" and silently assume the Hausdorff case.
  107	
  108	## Remarks
  109	
  110	**What the name promises is proved, not assumed.** That $X^{*}$ is compact, that
  111	$X$ sits inside it as an open subspace carrying its own topology, and the exact
  112	conditions under which $X$ is dense in $X^{*}$ or $X^{*}$ is Hausdorff, are
  113	[[thm-one-point-compactification-properties]]. Nothing above uses any of them.
  114	
  115	**The added point is a genuine construction and not a choice.** The set $\infty$
  116	above is determined by $X$; no appeal to any principle of choice is made, and no
  117	"take a point not in $X$" is left unexplained.
  118	
  119	**$X^{*}$ is Hausdorff exactly when $X$ is locally compact and Hausdorff**
  120	([[def-locally-compact-space]]), which is the reason local compactness and this
  121	construction always appear together.
FILE items/thm-heine-borel-r.md
    1	---
    2	id: thm-heine-borel-r
    3	kind: theorem
    4	title: "Heine-Borel by bisection: every closed bounded interval $[a,b]$ is compact"
    5	status: published
    6	origin: session
    7	deps: [def-open-cover-r, def-interval, thm-nested-interval-property, def-complete-ordered-field, thm-recursion, cor-archimedean-reciprocal, thm-of-archimedean, def-neighbourhood-r, def-open-and-closed-in-r, lem-of-naturals-positive, lem-of-inverse-positive, lem-of-abs-value, lem-of-add-order, lem-of-sign-rules, cor-of-one-positive, def-ordered-field]
    8	justified_by: []
    9	aliases: []
   10	landmark: true
   11	short: "$[a,b]$ is compact"
   12	proof_strategy: contradiction
   13	verification:
   14	  precheck: pass
   15	  judge:
   16	    model: z-ai/glm-5.2
   17	    verdict: pass
   18	    date: 2026-07-26
   19	  audited: 2026-07-26
   20	sources:
   21	  scraped: []
   22	  references:
   23	    - title: "Heine-Borel theorem (Wikipedia)"
   24	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heine%E2%80%93Borel_theorem"
   25	    - title: "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Ch. 2 (Thm 2.40)"
   26	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principles_of_Mathematical_Analysis"
   27	    - title: "J. Lebl, Basic Analysis I, §7.4"
   28	      url: "https://www.jirka.org/ra/"
   29	    - title: "Nested intervals (Wikipedia)"
   30	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nested_intervals"
   31	pipeline_run: null
   32	---
   33	
   34	## Statement
   35	
   36	Let $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a \le b$. Then the closed bounded interval
   37	$[a,b]$ ([[def-interval]]) is compact ([[def-open-cover-r]]): every family of
   38	open subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ whose union contains $[a,b]$ has a finite subfamily
   39	whose union already contains $[a,b]$.
   40	
   41	The proof is by repeated bisection. Supposing some open cover admits no finite
   42	subcover, one halves the interval, keeps a half that still admits none, and
   43	iterates; the halves shrink to a point, which the cover does reach, and a single
   44	member of the cover then swallows a whole late-stage half. **The halving rule is
   45	canonical**, taking the left half whenever the left half works, so the recursion
   46	uses [[thm-recursion]] and no choice principle.
   47	
   48	## Facts & Assumptions
   49	
   50	**Given:** Reals $a \le b$ and an open cover $\mathcal{U}$ of $[a,b]$; the set $X := \{\, (u,v) \in \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} : u \le v \,\}$; and the following terminology: a pair $(u,v) \in X$ is **bad** when there are no $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $[u,v] \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$, that is, when the interval $[u,v]$ admits no finite subcover from $\mathcal{U}$.
   51	
   52	[L1] Open cover, subcover, finite subfamily and compactness ([[def-open-cover-r]]).
   53	
   54	[L2] Closed bounded intervals: $[u,v] = \{\, z : u \le z \le v \,\}$ is nonempty exactly when $u \le v$; and for $u \le m \le v$ one has $[u,v] = [u,m] \cup [m,v]$, since $z \in [u,v]$ satisfies $z \le m$ or $m < z$ by trichotomy ([[def-interval]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
   55	
   56	[L3] $U$ is open when every $x \in U$ admits $\varepsilon > 0$ with $N_\varepsilon(x) \subseteq U$, and $N_\varepsilon(x) = \{\, y : |y - x| < \varepsilon \,\}$ ([[def-open-and-closed-in-r]], [[def-neighbourhood-r]]).
   57	
   58	[L4] Recursion: for a set $Y$, an element $y_0 \in Y$ and a function $G : Y \to Y$ there is $h : \mathbb{N} \to Y$ with $h(0) = y_0$ and $h(k+1) = G(h(k))$ for every $k$ ([[thm-recursion]]).
   59	
   60	[L5] Nested interval property: if $[a_k,b_k]$ with $a_k \le b_k$ satisfy $[a_{k+1},b_{k+1}] \subseteq [a_k,b_k]$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$, then $\bigcap_{k} [a_k,b_k] \ne \varnothing$ ([[thm-nested-interval-property]]).
   61	
   62	[L6] Reciprocal Archimedean property: for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a natural $n \ge 1$ with $1/n < \varepsilon$ ([[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]], [[thm-of-archimedean]]).
   63	
   64	[L7] Canonical naturals: $n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}} > 0$ for $n \ge 1$, the map $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$ is strictly increasing, and $(n+1) \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}} = n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}} + 1$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]); a positive element has a positive inverse and $0 < c \le d$ gives $0 < 1/d \le 1/c$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]]). These order-arithmetic facts are stated by their sources for the strict order only; the nonstrict forms used below follow by adjoining the equality case, in which the two sides coincide.
   65	
   66	[L8] Ordered-field arithmetic: $0 < 1$, hence $2 := 1 + 1 > 0$ and $2^{-1} > 0$; adding a constant preserves an inequality and multiplying by a positive preserves it ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[lem-of-add-order]], [[lem-of-sign-rules]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]). These order-arithmetic facts are stated by their sources for the strict order only; the nonstrict forms used below follow by adjoining the equality case, in which the two sides coincide.
   67	
   68	[L9] Absolute value: $|y - x| \le v - u$ whenever $x, y \in [u,v]$, because $|y - x|$ equals $y - x$ or $x - y$ and each is at most $v - u$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
   69	
   70	## Proof
   71	
   72	**Proof technique:** contradiction.
   73	
   74	1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $[a,b]$ is not compact: some open cover $\mathcal{U}$ of $[a,b]$ has no finite subcover, that is, the pair $(a,b) \in X$ is bad. [assume-contra, L1]
   75	
   76	1.2 Bisection rule: for $(u,v) \in X$ put $m := (u+v) \cdot 2^{-1}$, so that $u \le m \le v$ by [L8], and define $G(u,v) := (u,m)$ if $(u,m)$ is bad and $G(u,v) := (m,v)$ otherwise. This is a definition by cases on one condition, so $G : X \to X$ is a function and nothing is selected. [L2, L8, construct]
   77	
   78	1.3 If $(u,v)$ is bad then $G(u,v)$ is bad: were both $(u,m)$ and $(m,v)$ not bad, concatenating the two finite lists of members of $\mathcal{U}$ would give a finite subfamily whose union contains $[u,m] \cup [m,v] = [u,v]$ by [L2], so $(u,v)$ would not be bad; hence at least one half is bad, and the rule returns the left half when it is bad and otherwise the right half, which must then be bad. [L1, L2]
   79	
   80	2.1 Apply [L4] with $Y = X$, seed $(a,b)$ and map $G$: there is $h : \mathbb{N} \to X$ with $h(0) = (a,b)$ and $h(k+1) = G(h(k))$. Write $h(k) = (a_k,b_k)$, so $a_k \le b_k$ for every $k$, $(a_0,b_0) = (a,b)$, and $(a_{k+1},b_{k+1})$ is one of the two halves of $(a_k,b_k)$. [step 1.2, L2, L4, construct]
   81	
   82	3.1 Every $(a_k,b_k)$ is bad, by induction on $k$: the case $k = 0$ is step 1.1, and if $(a_k,b_k)$ is bad then $(a_{k+1},b_{k+1}) = G(a_k,b_k)$ is bad by step 1.3. [step 1.1, step 1.3, step 2.1]
   83	
   84	3.2 Writing $\ell_k := b_k - a_k \ge 0$, the intervals are nested and the lengths halve: $[a_{k+1},b_{k+1}]$ is $[a_k,m_k]$ or $[m_k,b_k]$ with $m_k = (a_k+b_k) \cdot 2^{-1}$, and each of these is contained in $[a_k,b_k]$ by [L2], while $m_k - a_k = b_k - m_k = \ell_k \cdot 2^{-1}$, so $\ell_{k+1} = \ell_k \cdot 2^{-1}$. [step 2.1, L2, L8]
   85	
   86	4.1 For every $k \in \mathbb{N}$ one has $\ell_k \cdot (k+1) \le \ell_0$, by induction on $k$: at $k = 0$ this reads $\ell_0 \cdot 1 \le \ell_0$; and if it holds at $k$ then $\ell_{k+1} \cdot (k+2) = \ell_k \cdot 2^{-1} \cdot (k+2) \le \ell_k \cdot (k+1) \le \ell_0$, using $\ell_k \ge 0$ and $(k+2) \cdot 2^{-1} \le k+1$, which is $k + 2 \le 2k+2$, that is $0 \le k$. [step 3.2, L7, L8]
   87	
   88	4.2 By [L5] the nested family $[a_k,b_k]$ of nonempty closed bounded intervals has a common point $x$; since $x \in [a_0,b_0] = [a,b]$ and $\mathcal{U}$ covers $[a,b]$, fix $U \in \mathcal{U}$ with $x \in U$ and then, $U$ being open, a real $\varepsilon > 0$ with $N_\varepsilon(x) \subseteq U$. [step 3.2, L1, L2, L3, L5, choose]
   89	
   90	5.1 There is $k \in \mathbb{N}$ with $\ell_k < \varepsilon$: the real $\varepsilon/(\ell_0+1)$ is positive because $\ell_0 + 1 > 0$, so [L6] supplies a natural $n \ge 1$ with $1/n < \varepsilon/(\ell_0+1)$; put $k := n-1$, a natural number, so that $k+1 = n$ and step 4.1 with [L7] gives $\ell_k \le \ell_0 \cdot (1/n) \le \ell_0 \cdot \varepsilon/(\ell_0+1) < \varepsilon$, the last step because $\ell_0 < \ell_0 + 1$ forces $\ell_0/(\ell_0+1) < 1$ and $\varepsilon > 0$. [step 4.1, step 4.2, L6, L7, L8, choose]
   91	
   92	6.1 For that $k$ one has $x \in [a_k,b_k]$, and every $y \in [a_k,b_k]$ satisfies $|y - x| \le \ell_k < \varepsilon$ by [L9], so $[a_k,b_k] \subseteq N_\varepsilon(x) \subseteq U$; hence the one-member subfamily $\{U\}$ of $\mathcal{U}$ covers $[a_k,b_k]$ and $(a_k,b_k)$ is not bad, contradicting step 3.1. The assumption of step 1.1 is therefore untenable and $[a,b]$ is compact. [step 3.1, step 4.2, step 5.1, L1, L3, L9, discharge-contradiction] ∎
   93	
   94	## Remarks
   95	
   96	- **What each hypothesis buys.** Closedness enters through [L5]: the nested
   97	  interval property is stated for closed intervals and fails for open ones
   98	  ([[cex-nested-open-intervals-empty]]). Boundedness enters through the same
   99	  fact and through the length computation of step 3.2. Completeness of
  100	  $\mathbb{R}$ enters only inside [[thm-nested-interval-property]] and, through
  101	  [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]], in step 5.1.
  102	
  103	- **Why the lengths are handled without powers.** The obvious route is
  104	  $\ell_k = \ell_0 \cdot 2^{-k}$ together with the nullity of a geometric
  105	  sequence, which is available ([[lem-geometric-sequence-null]]). The route
  106	  taken instead, the one-line induction of step 4.1, gives the weaker bound
  107	  $\ell_k \le \ell_0/(k+1)$, which is all step 5.1 needs, and it avoids
  108	  integer powers and the algebra of limits entirely.
  109	
  110	- **The recursion is over pairs, not over sets.** The state carried from stage
  111	  to stage is the pair of endpoints, so [L4] applies with $Y = X$ and a total
  112	  map $G$; had the rule been "choose a bad half", the state would have been
  113	  chosen rather than computed and the argument would have needed dependent
  114	  choice, which this library does not have.
  115	
  116	- **The converse direction is a separate result.** That a compact subset of
  117	  $\mathbb{R}$ must be closed and bounded is
  118	  [[lem-compact-implies-closed-and-bounded-r]], and the two together give
  119	  [[thm-heine-borel-characterisation-r]].
