   26	      url: "https://www.jirka.org/ra/"
   27	---
   28	
   29	## Definition
   30	
   31	Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field
   32	([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]) with its order
   33	([[def-real-order]]).
   34	
   35	A subset $I \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is **order-convex** when
   36	
   37	$$x, y \in I \text{ and } x \le z \le y \;\Longrightarrow\; z \in I .$$
   38	
   39	The **intervals** of $\mathbb{R}$ are the sets of the following nine forms, where
   40	$a, b \in \mathbb{R}$:
   41	
   42	| | bounded forms | | one-sided and full forms |
   43	|---|---|---|---|
   44	| $(a,b)$ | $\{x : a < x < b\}$ | $(a,\infty)$ | $\{x : a < x\}$ |
   45	| $[a,b]$ | $\{x : a \le x \le b\}$ | $[a,\infty)$ | $\{x : a \le x\}$ |
   46	| $[a,b)$ | $\{x : a \le x < b\}$ | $(-\infty,b)$ | $\{x : x < b\}$ |
   47	| $(a,b]$ | $\{x : a < x \le b\}$ | $(-\infty,b]$ | $\{x : x \le b\}$ |
   48	| | | $(-\infty,\infty)$ | $\mathbb{R}$ |
   49	
   50	An interval is **open** when both of its written endpoints are excluded, that is
   51	for the forms $(a,b)$, $(a,\infty)$, $(-\infty,b)$ and $(-\infty,\infty)$; it is
   52	**closed** when both written endpoints are included, that is for $[a,b]$,
   53	$[a,\infty)$, $(-\infty,b]$ and $(-\infty,\infty)$. The forms $[a,b)$ and
   54	$(a,b]$ are **half-open**.
   55	
   56	**The symbols $\pm\infty$ are notation and not elements of $\mathbb{R}$.** They
   57	mark which side carries no endpoint condition at all; the five forms in the right
   58	column are defined by the displayed conditions on $x$ alone, and no arithmetic is
   59	ever performed with $\infty$. This is the same refusal to extend $\mathbb{R}$
   60	silently that [[rem-sup-conventions]] records for suprema.
   61	
   62	**Every one of the nine forms is order-convex.** Each is defined by a conjunction
   63	of at most two conditions, each of the shape $a < x$, $a \le x$, $x < b$ or
   64	$x \le b$, and each such condition is inherited by an intermediate point: if
   65	$a < x$ and $x \le z$ then $a < z$, and if $z \le y$ and $y < b$ then $z < b$,
   66	by transitivity of the order ([[def-ordered-field]]). Applying this to whichever
   67	one or two conditions define the form in question gives $z \in I$ whenever
   68	$x, y \in I$ and $x \le z \le y$.
   69	
   70	**Bounded intervals.** An interval is **bounded** ([[def-bounded-set]]) exactly
   71	when it is of one of the four forms in the left column: for those, $a$ is a lower
   72	bound and $b$ an upper bound. The other five forms are unbounded, on the side or
   73	sides written with $\infty$; the verification is in the remarks below.
   74	
   75	**Nondegeneracy.** An interval is **degenerate** when it has at most one element,
   76	and **nondegenerate** when it has at least two. For the four bounded forms with
   77	endpoints $a$ and $b$:
   78	
   79	- $[a,b]$ is nonempty exactly when $a \le b$, and it is nondegenerate exactly
   80	  when $a < b$. It is the singleton $\{a\}$ when $a = b$.
   81	- $(a,b)$, $[a,b)$ and $(a,b]$ are nonempty exactly when $a < b$, and then each
   82	  is nondegenerate.
   83	
   84	The only assertion here that is not immediate from the defining conditions is
   85	that $a < b$ makes $(a,b)$ nonempty with at least two points. It holds because
   86	$a < (a+b)/2 < b$, which follows from $a < b$ by adding $a$, respectively $b$, to
   87	both sides and halving ([[def-ordered-field]]); repeating the halving inside
   88	$(a, (a+b)/2)$ produces a second point.
   89	
   90	**Closed bounded intervals.** These are the sets $[a,b]$ with $a \le b$, which is
   91	exactly the condition making them nonempty. They are the intervals the nested
   92	interval property is stated for, and the phrase *closed bounded interval* always
   93	carries the hypothesis $a \le b$ in this library.
   94	
   95	**Length.** The **length** of a bounded interval presented by its endpoints
   96	$a \le b$ is
   97	
   98	$$\ell = b - a \;\ge\; 0 .$$
   99	
  100	Length is attached to the presentation by endpoints and is not recovered from the
  101	set: $(a,b)$, $[a,b)$ and $(a,b]$ are all empty when $a = b$, and so is
  102	$(c,c)$ for any other $c$, while each of these presentations has length $0$, so
  103	nothing inconsistent arises; but the endpoints are named explicitly at every
  104	point where a length is used in this library, and never inferred from the set.
  105	Unbounded intervals are assigned no length.
  106	
  107	## Remarks
  108	
  109	- **Why the five unbounded forms really are unbounded.** Take $[a,\infty)$ and
  110	  suppose $u \in \mathbb{R}$ were an upper bound of it. The element
  111	  $x = a + |u - a| + 1$ satisfies $x \ge a$, so $x \in [a,\infty)$, and
  112	  $x \ge a + (u - a) + 1 = u + 1 > u$, since $|t| \ge t$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]])
  113	  and $1 > 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]). That contradicts $x \le u$. The same
  114	  computation with $a$ replaced by any element of $(a,\infty)$ handles the open
  115	  form, and reflecting through the origin handles $(-\infty,b)$ and
  116	  $(-\infty,b]$; $\mathbb{R}$ itself is unbounded on both sides for the same
  117	  reason. Note that this uses no Archimedean property: it is the failure of a
  118	  *single* bound, not the cofinality of the naturals.
  119	
  120	- **The converse classification is not asserted here.** It is true that every
  121	  order-convex subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is empty or one of the nine forms, and the
  122	  proof runs through suprema and infima, but nothing in this library needs it and
  123	  it is not proved anywhere here. What is used is only the direction proved
  124	  above: each of the nine forms is order-convex.
  125	
  126	- **Degenerate intervals are kept, not excluded.** $[a,a] = \{a\}$ and
  127	  $(a,a) = \emptyset$ are intervals under this definition. Excluding them would
  128	  force a nonemptiness hypothesis into every statement that produces an interval,
  129	  and the nested interval property is a good illustration: its conclusion is that
  130	  the intersection is nonempty, and in the equality case the intersection is the
  131	  degenerate interval $[a,a]$, which is exactly the single point.
