   41	
   42	## Definition
   43	
   44	A set $\alpha$ is an **ordinal** when both of the following hold.
   45	
   46	1. $\alpha$ is a **transitive set**: every element of $\alpha$ is also a subset
   47	   of $\alpha$, that is $x \in \alpha \Rightarrow x \subseteq \alpha$.
   48	2. The membership relation restricted to $\alpha$, namely
   49	   $\{(x, y) \in \alpha \times \alpha : x \in y\}$, is a **strict well-order**
   50	   of $\alpha$ ([[def-well-order]]): it is irreflexive, transitive as a
   51	   relation, trichotomous on $\alpha$, and every nonempty subset of $\alpha$ has
   52	   an $\in$-least element.
   53	
   54	Ordinals are written with lowercase Greek letters, and for ordinals we set
   55	
   56	$$\alpha < \beta :\iff \alpha \in \beta, \qquad \alpha \le \beta :\iff (\alpha \in \beta \text{ or } \alpha = \beta).$$
   57	
   58	Write $0 := \emptyset$, which is an ordinal because both clauses hold vacuously,
   59	and write $\alpha^{+} := \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ for the **successor** of
   60	$\alpha$.
   61	
   62	## Remarks
   63	
   64	- **Two different transitivities.** Clause 1 is about the *set* $\alpha$: it
   65	  contains all members of its members. Clause 2 asks in part that the *relation*
   66	  $\in$ be transitive on $\alpha$. If every element of $\alpha$ is itself a
   67	  transitive set then the relation $\in$ is transitive on $\alpha$; the converse
   68	  holds *given clause 1*, and fails without it. For
   69	  $\alpha = \{\{\{\emptyset\}\}\}$ the relation
   70	  $\{(x, y) \in \alpha \times \alpha : x \in y\}$ is empty, hence vacuously
   71	  transitive, yet the sole element $\{\{\emptyset\}\}$ of $\alpha$ is not a
   72	  transitive set, since $\{\emptyset\} \in \{\{\emptyset\}\}$ while
   73	  $\emptyset \notin \{\{\emptyset\}\}$. Under clause 1 the two readings do
   74	  coincide, because then every member of a member of $\alpha$ again lies in
   75	  $\alpha$, where the relation has something to say. Neither clause implies the
   76	  other, and both are needed.
   77	- **Every ordinal is literally the set of all smaller ordinals.** By clause 1 and
   78	  the notation above, $\alpha = \{\beta : \beta < \alpha\}$, so the ordinals
   79	  carry their own order relation as membership. This is von Neumann's device,
   80	  and it is what makes it unnecessary to define an order type as an equivalence
   81	  class of well-orders: apart from the class of the empty well-order, which is the
   82	  singleton $\{\varnothing\}$, such a class is a proper class rather than a set,
   83	  whereas the representative chosen here is always a set.
   84	- **The natural numbers are ordinals, and so is $\omega$.** Each natural number
   85	  is a transitive set ([[lem-nat-transitive-irreflexive]]), and on $\mathbb{N}$
   86	  membership coincides with the usual strict order, so each natural number and
   87	  $\mathbb{N} = \omega$ itself ([[def-natural-numbers]]) satisfy both clauses.
   88	  Both facts are proved in [[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]], where they are
   89	  needed; they are recorded here because they are the intended picture.
   90	- **The Axiom of Foundation is not used.** Some treatments define an ordinal as a
   91	  transitive set *linearly* ordered by $\in$, which is equivalent to the
   92	  definition above only in the presence of Foundation, since Foundation is what
   93	  supplies the least element. This library does not assume Foundation anywhere,
   94	  so the least element property is written into the definition instead.
   95	- The definition is absolute in a strong sense: whether a set is an ordinal
   96	  depends only on its members and the membership relation among them, with no
   97	  reference to any ambient construction. That is why ordinals can be used to
   98	  index constructions in any model of ZF without further hypotheses.
