   33	
   34	## Definition
   35	
   36	Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be ordinals ([[def-ordinal]]). The **sum**
   37	$\alpha + \beta$ is defined by recursion on $\beta$, in the three cases of
   38	[[def-limit-ordinal]]:
   39	
   40	$$\alpha + 0 = \alpha, \qquad \alpha + \beta^{+} = (\alpha + \beta)^{+}, \qquad \alpha + \lambda = \bigcup\{\, \alpha + \beta : \beta \in \lambda \,\} \quad (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}).$$
   41	
   42	That exactly one operation satisfies these three clauses, and that all its
   43	values are ordinals, is [[cor-ordinal-addition-well-defined]], proved
   44	immediately above. The union in the limit clause is the least upper bound of the
   45	values already produced (claim (e) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]]), so it may be
   46	written $\sup$ and the clause read as "at a limit, take the supremum".
   47	
   48	**Notation.** We write $1 = 0^{+}$, $2 = 1^{+}$, and so on for the finite
   49	ordinals, and $\sup$ for $\bigcup$ applied to a set of ordinals. The successor
   50	operation is now a special case of addition:
   51	
   52	$$\alpha + 1 = \alpha + 0^{+} = (\alpha + 0)^{+} = \alpha^{+},$$
   53	
   54	so from here on $\alpha + 1$ and $\alpha^{+}$ denote the same ordinal, and both
   55	notations are used, whichever reads better.
   56	
   57	## Remarks
   58	
   59	- **The recursion is on the right argument only.** The left argument $\alpha$ is
   60	  a parameter, frozen before the recursion starts. This asymmetry is not an
   61	  artefact of the presentation: ordinal addition is genuinely asymmetric, and
   62	  the asymmetry is exactly what [[fs-ordinal-addition-is-commutative]] and
   63	  [[fs-ordinal-addition-is-strictly-monotone-in-the-left-argument]] exhibit
   64	  later on this page.
   65	
   66	- **What the clauses say concretely.** $\alpha + \beta$ is "$\alpha$, and then
   67	  $\beta$ more steps". [[lem-ordinal-sum-as-an-order-type]] turns that picture
   68	  into a theorem: $\alpha + \beta$ is the order type of a copy of $\alpha$
   69	  followed by a copy of $\beta$. The order-type description is usually the one
   70	  to compute with; the recursion is the one that makes the definition legitimate.
   71	
   72	- **The first interesting value.** $\omega$ is the least limit ordinal
   73	  ([[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]]), and it is the least ordinal at which the
   74	  third clause fires at all: below $\omega$ every ordinal is $0$ or a successor,
   75	  so below $\omega$ this recursion is literally the Peano recursion for
   76	  addition on $\mathbb{N}$. That the two agree there is
   77	  [[thm-ordinal-arithmetic-agrees-on-omega]], and it is a theorem, not a
   78	  convention.
   79	
   80	- **Suprema need no completeness axiom.** The limit clause takes the union of a
   81	  set of ordinals, which is an ordinal and is their least upper bound, by claim
   82	  (e) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]] and the remark following it. Nothing resembling
   83	  the least upper bound property of $\mathbb{R}$ is assumed; it is a closure
   84	  property of the ordinals themselves.
   85	
   86	- **Left addition of a limit is a limit.** For a limit $\lambda$, the values
   87	  $\alpha + \beta$ for $\beta \in \lambda$ are strictly increasing and their
   88	  supremum is not attained, so $\alpha + \lambda$ is again a limit ordinal. This
   89	  is recorded as a clause of [[thm-ordinal-arithmetic-monotonicity]], where it
   90	  is proved.
