   43	
   44	## Statement
   45	
   46	Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$. A von Neumann natural is the set of its
   47	predecessors, $n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), so it can be
   48	used directly as an index set. Define
   49	
   50	$$\mathbb{R}^n := \{\, x : x \text{ is a function } n \to \mathbb{R} \,\},$$
   51	
   52	and write $x_k$ for $x(k)$, $k < n$. Two elements of $\mathbb{R}^n$ are equal
   53	exactly when they agree at every $k < n$, functions being equal when they have
   54	the same values. For $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ put
   55	
   56	$$d_1(x,y) := \sum_{k<n} |x_k - y_k|, \qquad d_2(x,y) := \sqrt{\ \sum_{k<n} (x_k - y_k)^2\ }, \qquad d_\infty(x,y) := \max\{\, |x_k - y_k| : k < n \,\}.$$
   57	
   58	All three are well defined: the finite sums are those of [[def-finite-sum]]; the
   59	sum of squares is nonnegative ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[lem-of-square-positive]])
   60	so it has a unique nonnegative square root ([[thm-of-square-roots]]); and
   61	$\{|x_k - y_k| : k < n\}$ is a nonempty finite subset of $\mathbb{R}$, because
   62	$n \ge 1$, so it has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).
   63	
   64	**Then $d_1$, $d_2$ and $d_\infty$ are metrics on $\mathbb{R}^n$**
   65	([[def-metric-space]]).
   66	
   67	**Why $n \ge 1$.** For $n = 0$ the set $\mathbb{R}^0$ has exactly one element,
   68	the empty function, and $d_1$ and $d_2$ are the empty sum $0$ and its root; but
   69	$d_\infty$ would be the maximum of the empty set, which does not exist. The
   70	hypothesis $n \ge 1$ is therefore not decoration, and it is carried by every
   71	statement about $d_\infty$ in this library.
   72	
   73	## Facts & Assumptions
   74	
   75	**Given:** A natural $n \ge 1$; elements $x, y, z \in \mathbb{R}^n$; and the lists $a_k := x_k - y_k$, $b_k := y_k - z_k$ for $k < n$, so that $a_k + b_k = x_k - z_k$. Write $A := \sum_{k<n} a_k^2$, $C := \sum_{k<n} b_k^2$ and $B := \sum_{k<n} a_k b_k$.
   76	
   77	[L1] Laws of finite sums ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[def-finite-sum]]): additivity, scaling, monotonicity; a sum of nonnegative terms is nonnegative, every single term is at most the sum, and a sum of nonnegative terms that vanishes has every term $0$.
   78	
   79	[L2] Absolute value ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-abs-value]]): $|u| \ge 0$; $|u| = 0$ if and only if $u = 0$; $|-u| = |u|$; and $u \le |u|$.
   80	
   81	[L3] Two-term triangle inequality: $|u + v| \le |u| + |v|$ ([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]]).
   82	
   83	[L4] Minkowski's inequality at the rational exponent $p = 1$ ([[thm-minkowski-finite]]): $\sum_{k<n}|a_k + b_k| \le \sum_{k<n}|a_k| + \sum_{k<n}|b_k|$.
   84	
   85	[L5] Cauchy-Schwarz in root form ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]]): $\big|\sum_{k<n} a_k b_k\big| \le \sqrt{\sum_{k<n} a_k^2}\ \sqrt{\sum_{k<n} b_k^2}$.
   86	
   87	[L6] Square roots ([[thm-of-square-roots]]): every $c \ge 0$ has a unique $\sqrt{c} \ge 0$ with $(\sqrt{c})^2 = c$; in particular $\sqrt{c} = 0$ if and only if $c = 0$.
   88	
   89	[L7] Squares ([[lem-of-square-positive]], [[def-integer-power]]): $u^2 \ge 0$ always, and $u^2 = 0$ only for $u = 0$; and monotonicity of squaring on the nonnegatives, $s \le t \iff s^2 \le t^2$ for $s, t \ge 0$ ([[lem-of-square-monotone]]).
   90	
   91	[L8] Maximum of a nonempty finite set of reals: it exists, it belongs to the set, and it is an upper bound of the set ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).
   92	
   93	[L9] Order arithmetic in $\mathbb{R}$: inequalities may be added and a constant added to both sides, in the strict form of [[lem-of-add-order]] and, together with the case of equality settled by totality ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]), in the nonstrict form used below.
   94	
   95	## Proof
   96	
   97	**Proof technique:** direct.
   98	
   99	1.1 Separation for $d_1$: $d_1(x,y) = \sum_{k<n}|a_k|$ is a sum of nonnegative terms, so it vanishes exactly when every $|a_k|$ vanishes, that is exactly when $x_k = y_k$ for all $k < n$, that is exactly when $x = y$. [L1, L2]
  100	
  101	1.2 Separation for $d_2$: $d_2(x,y) = \sqrt{A}$ vanishes exactly when $A = 0$; $A$ is a sum of nonnegative terms, so $A = 0$ exactly when $a_k^2 = 0$ for every $k < n$, which happens exactly when every $a_k = 0$, that is exactly when $x = y$. [L1, L6, L7]
  102	
  103	1.3 Separation for $d_\infty$: the maximum $d_\infty(x,y)$ belongs to $\{|a_k| : k < n\}$ and bounds it above, so it is $0$ exactly when every $|a_k| = 0$, that is exactly when $x = y$. [L2, L8]
  104	
  105	1.4 Symmetry for all three: $|y_k - x_k| = |-(x_k - y_k)| = |x_k - y_k|$ and $(y_k - x_k)^2 = (x_k - y_k)^2$ for every $k < n$, so the three defining expressions are unchanged when $x$ and $y$ are exchanged. [L2, L7]
  106	
  107	1.5 Triangle inequality for $d_1$: applying [L4] to the lists $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ gives $d_1(x,z) = \sum_{k<n}|a_k + b_k| \le \sum_{k<n}|a_k| + \sum_{k<n}|b_k| = d_1(x,y) + d_1(y,z)$. [L4]
  108	
  109	1.6 Expanding with additivity and scaling: $\sum_{k<n}(a_k + b_k)^2 = \sum_{k<n}\big(a_k^2 + 2a_kb_k + b_k^2\big) = A + 2B + C$. [L1, algebra]
  110	
  111	1.7 By [L5] and $B \le |B|$: $B \le \sqrt{A}\,\sqrt{C}$, and $A = (\sqrt{A})^2$, $C = (\sqrt{C})^2$ with $\sqrt{A}, \sqrt{C} \ge 0$. [L2, L5, L6]
  112	
  113	1.8 Triangle inequality for $d_\infty$: for each $k < n$, $|a_k + b_k| \le |a_k| + |b_k| \le d_\infty(x,y) + d_\infty(y,z)$ because the two maxima bound their sets; so $d_\infty(x,y) + d_\infty(y,z)$ is an upper bound of $\{|a_k + b_k| : k < n\}$, and the maximum $d_\infty(x,z)$ of that set is one of its elements, whence $d_\infty(x,z) \le d_\infty(x,y) + d_\infty(y,z)$. [L3, L8, L9]
  114	
  115	2.1 Combining steps 1.6 and 1.7: $\sum_{k<n}(a_k+b_k)^2 = A + 2B + C \le (\sqrt{A})^2 + 2\sqrt{A}\sqrt{C} + (\sqrt{C})^2 = \big(\sqrt{A} + \sqrt{C}\big)^2$. [step 1.6, step 1.7, L9, algebra]
  116	
  117	3.1 Both $d_2(x,z) = \sqrt{\sum_{k<n}(a_k+b_k)^2}$ and $\sqrt{A} + \sqrt{C}$ are nonnegative, and by step 2.1 the square of the first is at most the square of the second, so monotonicity of squaring on the nonnegatives gives $d_2(x,z) \le \sqrt{A} + \sqrt{C} = d_2(x,y) + d_2(y,z)$. [step 2.1, L6, L7]
  118	
  119	4.1 Each of $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\infty$ satisfies (M1) by steps 1.1, 1.2 and 1.3, satisfies (M2) by step 1.4, and satisfies (M3) by steps 1.5, 3.1 and 1.8 respectively; hence all three are metrics on $\mathbb{R}^n$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 1.5, step 1.8, step 3.1] ∎
  120	
  121	## Remarks
  122	
  123	- **$\mathbb{R}^n$ is defined ZFC-natively here**, as the set of functions from
  124	  the von Neumann natural $n$ to $\mathbb{R}$, precisely so that its coordinates
  125	  are indexed by $k < n$ and the finite-sum machinery of [[def-finite-sum]],
  126	  [[thm-minkowski-finite]] and [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]], all of which sum
  127	  over $k < n$, applies without any reindexing.
  128	- **No rational power appears anywhere above.** The triangle inequality for
  129	  $d_2$ is obtained from Cauchy-Schwarz and the existence of square roots, not
  130	  from Minkowski at $p = 2$, so this lemma does not depend on the theory of
  131	  rational exponents. Minkowski is used only at $p = 1$, where its statement is
  132	  the termwise sum of the two-term triangle inequality.
  133	- **The three metrics are Lipschitz equivalent, with explicit constants,** and
  134	  in particular have the same topology; that computation is on the companion
  135	  page and is not needed here.
