   38	---
   39	
   40	## Statement
   41	
   42	Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$, and give $\mathbb{R}$ its usual topology,
   43	the metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$
   44	([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metrizable-space]]). Let
   45	
   46	$$\mathbb{R}^n \;=\; \prod_{k < n} \mathbb{R}$$
   47	
   48	be the product of $n$ copies of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-product-topology]]). As a
   49	**set** this is literally the $\mathbb{R}^n$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], both being
   50	the set of functions $n \to \mathbb{R}$; and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\infty$ are the
   51	three metrics defined there. Then:
   52	
   53	1. The product topology on $\mathbb{R}^n$ is the metric topology of $d_\infty$
   54	   ([[def-metric-topology]]). The key computation is that a $d_\infty$-ball is a
   55	   box:
   56	   $$B_{d_\infty}(x, r) \;=\; \prod_{k<n} (x_k - r,\ x_k + r) \qquad (r > 0),$$
   57	   a product of bounded open intervals ([[def-interval]]).
   58	2. $d_\infty \le d_1 \le n\, d_\infty$ and $d_\infty \le d_2 \le n\, d_\infty$
   59	   pointwise, so $d_1$ and $d_2$ are each Lipschitz equivalent to $d_\infty$
   60	   ([[def-equivalent-metrics]]); here $n$ denotes the canonical natural
   61	   $n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$.
   62	3. Consequently all three metrics induce the product topology
   63	   ([[thm-metric-equivalence-hierarchy]]). So $\mathbb{R}^n$ carrying the product
   64	   topology and $\mathbb{R}^n$ carrying the topology of any one of $d_1$, $d_2$,
   65	   $d_\infty$ are **one topological space**, and it is metrizable
   66	   ([[def-metrizable-space]]).
   67	
   68	**Why $n \ge 1$.** The metric $d_\infty$ is a maximum over $n$ terms, which does
   69	not exist for $n = 0$; [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] carries the same hypothesis, and it
   70	is carried here for the same reason. For $n = 0$ the product is a one-point space
   71	and there is nothing to compare.
   72	
   73	## Facts & Assumptions
   74	
   75	**Given:** A natural $n \ge 1$; the set $\mathbb{R}^n$ of functions $n \to \mathbb{R}$; the three metrics $d_1(x,y) = \sum_{k<n}|x_k - y_k|$, $d_2(x,y) = \sqrt{\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^2}$ and $d_\infty(x,y) = \max\{|x_k-y_k| : k < n\}$; points $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ and a real $r > 0$. Throughout, $n$ inside a real inequality denotes the canonical natural $n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$.
   76	
   77	[A1] $d_1$, $d_2$ and $d_\infty$ are metrics on $\mathbb{R}^n$ for $n \ge 1$, and $\mathbb{R}^n$ is the set of functions $n \to \mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]).
   78	
   79	[A2] For $I = n$ a natural number, a basis for the product topology on $\prod_{k<n}\mathbb{R}$ is the family of all boxes $\prod_{k<n} U_k$ with every $U_k$ open in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
   80	
   81	[L1] $B_{\mathbb{R}}(t, r) = (t-r,\ t+r)$, and $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when every $t \in U$ has some $r > 0$ with $(t-r,t+r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claims 2 and 3; [[def-metric-ball]], [[def-metric-topology]], [[def-interval]]).
   82	
   83	[L2] $U$ is open in a metric space $(X,d)$ exactly when every $u \in U$ has some $\rho > 0$ with $B_d(u,\rho) \subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metric-ball]]); metric values are nonnegative ([[lem-metric-nonnegativity]]).
   84	
   85	[L3] $\max S$ belongs to $S$ and is an upper bound for $S$, and likewise $\min S$ ([[def-max-min]]); a nonempty finite set of reals has a maximum, and by reflection a minimum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]]).
   86	
   87	[L4] For finite sums: if $a_k \le b_k$ for all $k<n$ then $\sum_{k<n} a_k \le \sum_{k<n} b_k$; if every $a_k \ge 0$ then every single term satisfies $a_j \le \sum_{k<n} a_k$; and $\sum_{k<n}\lambda = n\lambda$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], claims 2 and 4).
   88	
   89	[L5] $\sqrt{a}$ is the unique nonnegative real with $(\sqrt a)^2 = a$, for $a \ge 0$ ([[thm-of-square-roots]]); $t^2 \ge 0$ ([[lem-of-square-positive]]); $|t|^2 = t^2$ and $|t| \ge 0$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]); and for $a, b \ge 0$ one has $a \le b$ if and only if $a^2 \le b^2$ ([[lem-of-square-monotone]]).
   90	
   91	[L6] A function on a natural number $n$ whose values are nonempty sets has a choice function for its family of values ([[lem-finite-choice]]).
   92	
   93	[L7] The canonical natural $n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$ is positive and $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_{\mathbb{R}}$ is strictly increasing for $n \ge 1$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]); multiplying an inequality by a positive element preserves it ([[lem-of-sign-rules]], claim 4).
   94	
   95	[L8] Lipschitz equivalent metrics are topologically equivalent, that is they have the same metric topology ([[thm-metric-equivalence-hierarchy]], claims 1 and 2; [[def-equivalent-metrics]]).
   96	
   97	## Proof
   98	
   99	**Proof technique:** direct.
  100	
  101	1.1 For $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$: $d_\infty(x,y) < r$ if and only if $|x_k - y_k| < r$ for every $k < n$, since by [L3] the maximum is one of the values $|x_k-y_k|$ and is an upper bound for all of them. [A1, L3]
  102	
  103	1.2 For $t \in \mathbb{R}$ and $r > 0$: $|t - y_k| < r$ if and only if $y_k \in (t-r, t+r)$, by [L1]. [L1]
  104	
  105	1.3 Write $t_k := |x_k - y_k|$ and $M := d_\infty(x,y) = \max\{t_k : k<n\}$. Then $t_j \le M$ for every $j < n$ and $M = t_{j_0}$ for some $j_0 < n$, by [L3]. [A1, L3]
  106	
  107	1.4 $d_2(x,y)^2 = \sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^2$ by [L5], and $(x_k-y_k)^2 = t_k^2 \ge 0$ by [L5]. [L5]
  108	
  109	1.5 $n \le n^2$ as reals: for $n \ge 1$ the canonical natural satisfies $\iota(1) = 1 \le \iota(n)$ by [L7], so either $\iota(n) = 1$, in which case $\iota(n) = \iota(n)^2 = 1$, or $1 < \iota(n)$, in which case multiplying that strict inequality by $\iota(n) > 0$ gives $\iota(n) < \iota(n)^2$ by [L7]. [L7]
  110	
  111	1.6 Conversely let $B = \prod_{k<n} U_k$ be a box with every $U_k$ open in $\mathbb{R}$ and let $x \in B$. For each $k<n$ the set $\{\, \rho \in \mathbb{R} : \rho > 0,\ (x_k-\rho,\ x_k+\rho) \subseteq U_k \,\}$ is nonempty by [L1], so [L6] supplies $\rho_k$ in it for every $k<n$; put $r := \min\{\rho_k : k<n\}$, which exists and is positive by [L3]. [A2, L1, L3, L6, choose]
  112	
  113	2.1 $d_1(x,y) = \sum_{k<n} t_k \le \sum_{k<n} M = n M$, using $t_k \le M$ from step 1.3 and [L4]. [step 1.3, L4]
  114	
  115	2.2 $M = t_{j_0} \le \sum_{k<n} t_k = d_1(x,y)$, since every $t_k \ge 0$ by [L5] and a single nonnegative term is at most the sum, by [L4]. [step 1.3, L4, L5]
  116	
  117	2.3 $B_{d_\infty}(x,r) = \prod_{k<n}(x_k - r,\ x_k + r)$: by step 1.1 a point $y$ lies in the ball exactly when $|x_k-y_k| < r$ for every $k<n$, and by step 1.2 that says exactly $y_k \in (x_k-r, x_k+r)$ for every $k < n$. [step 1.1, step 1.2]
  118	
  119	2.4 $M^2 = t_{j_0}^2 \le \sum_{k<n} t_k^2 = d_2(x,y)^2$ by steps 1.3 and 1.4 with [L4], and both $M$ and $d_2(x,y)$ are nonnegative by [L2] and [L5], so $M \le d_2(x,y)$ by [L5]. [step 1.3, step 1.4, L2, L4, L5]
  120	
  121	2.5 $d_2(x,y)^2 = \sum_{k<n} t_k^2 \le \sum_{k<n} M^2 = n M^2 \le n^2 M^2 = (nM)^2$, using $t_k \le M$ with [L5] and [L4], then step 1.5 with $M^2 \ge 0$; since $d_2(x,y) \ge 0$ and $nM \ge 0$, [L5] gives $d_2(x,y) \le n M$. [step 1.3, step 1.4, step 1.5, L4, L5]
  122	
  123	3.1 Every $d_\infty$-ball is a box with open factors, by step 2.3 and [L1], hence a basic open set of the product topology by [A2]; so every $d_\infty$-open set is product-open, by [L2] and [A2]. [step 2.3, A2, L1, L2]
  124	
  125	3.2 With $r$ as in step 1.6: $B_{d_\infty}(x,r) = \prod_{k<n}(x_k-r, x_k+r) \subseteq \prod_{k<n}(x_k-\rho_k, x_k+\rho_k) \subseteq B$, since $r \le \rho_k$ for every $k$ by [L3]. [step 2.3, step 1.6, L3]
  126	
  127	3.3 Steps 2.1, 2.2, 2.4 and 2.5 give $d_\infty \le d_1 \le n\,d_\infty$ and $d_\infty \le d_2 \le n\,d_\infty$ at every pair of points, which is claim 2, the constants $1$ and $n$ being positive by [L7]. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.4, step 2.5, L7]
  128	
  129	4.1 By steps 1.6 and 3.2 every basic open set of the product topology is $d_\infty$-open by [L2], hence every product-open set is $d_\infty$-open; with step 3.1 this gives claim 1. [step 3.1, step 1.6, step 3.2, A2, L2]
  130	
  131	5.1 By step 3.3 and [L8] the metrics $d_1$, $d_2$ and $d_\infty$ have the same metric topology, which by step 4.1 is the product topology; so all three induce it and $\mathbb{R}^n$ with the product topology is metrizable. This is claim 3, and with steps 4.1 and 3.3 all three claims are proved. [step 3.3, step 4.1, L8] ∎
  132	
  133	## Remarks
  134	
  135	- **This item exists to stop one symbol meaning two things.** Before it,
  136	  "$\mathbb{R}^2$" could denote the product of two copies of the real line or the
  137	  metric space of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], and "open in $\mathbb{R}^2$" would have
  138	  had two readings. Claim 3 says they are one space, so every statement about
  139	  open sets, closures, convergence and continuity in $\mathbb{R}^n$ proved on
  140	  either side transfers verbatim to the other.
  141	
  142	- **The $d_\infty$-ball is the natural object here and the $d_2$-ball is not.**
  143	  The proof works with $d_\infty$ because its balls *are* the basic boxes; for
  144	  $d_2$ the corresponding computation would need a round ball inscribed in a box
  145	  and a box inscribed in a round ball, which is the content of the inequalities
  146	  of claim 2 read geometrically.
  147	
  148	- **Choice is spent only on finitely many radii.** Step 1.6 selects one radius per
  149	  coordinate, and there are $n$ of them, so [[lem-finite-choice]] suffices and no
  150	  form of the Axiom of Choice is used anywhere in this item; step 3.2 only uses the
  151	  radius already built there.
