   15	verification:
   16	  precheck: n/a
   17	  judge:
   18	    model: z-ai/glm-5.2
   19	    verdict: pass
   20	    date: 2026-07-27
   21	  audited: 2026-07-27
   22	sources:
   23	  scraped: []
   24	  references:
   25	    - title: "Continuous function (Wikipedia)"
   26	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuous_function"
   27	    - title: "J. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., §18"
   28	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Munkres"
   29	pipeline_run: null
   30	---
   31	
   32	## Definition
   33	
   34	Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces
   35	([[def-topological-space]]), let $f : X \to Y$ be a function and let $x \in X$.
   36	Neighbourhoods are as in [[def-neighbourhood-top]].
   37	
   38	$f$ is **continuous at $x$** if for every neighbourhood $V$ of $f(x)$ in $Y$ the
   39	preimage $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ in $X$.
   40	
   41	$f$ is **continuous** if it is continuous at every point of $X$.
   42	
   43	**The same condition with open sets only.** $f$ is continuous at $x$ if and only
   44	if for every open $V \subseteq Y$ with $f(x) \in V$ there is an open
   45	$U \subseteq X$ with $x \in U$ and $f[U] \subseteq V$. Indeed, if $f$ is
   46	continuous at $x$ and $V$ is such an open set, then $V$ is a neighbourhood of
   47	$f(x)$, so $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$ and contains an open $U \ni x$,
   48	which satisfies $f[U] \subseteq V$. Conversely, given the displayed condition and
   49	a neighbourhood $V$ of $f(x)$, fix open $V_0$ with $f(x) \in V_0 \subseteq V$ and
   50	then open $U \ni x$ with $f[U] \subseteq V_0$; then $x \in U \subseteq f^{-1}[V_0] \subseteq f^{-1}[V]$,
   51	so $f^{-1}[V]$ is a neighbourhood of $x$. Both forms are used below and are the
   52	same statement written twice.
   53	
   54	**Preimage, not image.** $f^{-1}[V] = \{\, x \in X : f(x) \in V \,\}$
   55	is the preimage in the sense of [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]] and is
   56	defined for every function, invertible or not; no inverse function is being
   57	asserted to exist. Continuity is a condition on preimages throughout, and the
   58	corresponding conditions on images define the *open* and *closed* maps of a later
   59	item, which are different notions.
   60	
   61	## Remarks
   62	
   63	- **This is the metric definition when both topologies are metric topologies.**
   64	  For metric spaces, $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ continuity at $a$
   65	  ([[def-metric-continuity]]) says that every ball around $f(a)$ has a ball
   66	  around $a$ mapped into it, and the balls around a point are a neighbourhood
   67	  base there; the identification is carried out where metrizable spaces are
   68	  defined later on this page. Nothing about a metric survives in the definition
   69	  above: continuity is a relation between two topologies and a function, and it
   70	  is meaningless to ask whether a function between bare sets is continuous.
   71	
   72	- **Continuity depends on both topologies, and coarsening the target or refining
   73	  the source only helps.** If $f$ is continuous and $\mathcal{T}_X$ is replaced
   74	  by a finer topology, or $\mathcal{T}_Y$ by a coarser one, $f$ remains
   75	  continuous, since each condition to be verified is weakened and each
   76	  neighbourhood available in the source is still available. In particular every
   77	  map out of a discrete space and every map into an indiscrete space is
   78	  continuous ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
   79	
   80	- **Continuity at a point is strictly weaker than continuity.** A function may be
   81	  continuous at exactly one point, and the definition above is deliberately local
   82	  so that the sequential criteria proved later can be stated pointwise.
