   26	sources:
   27	  scraped: []
   28	  references:
   29	    - title: "Product topology (Wikipedia)"
   30	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Product_topology"
   31	    - title: "Axiom of choice (Wikipedia)"
   32	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_choice"
   33	    - title: "J. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., §19"
   34	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Munkres"
   35	pipeline_run: null
   36	---
   37	
   38	## Statement
   39	
   40	Let $(X_i, \mathcal{T}_i)_{i \in I}$ be topological spaces and let
   41	$P := \prod_{i \in I} X_i$ carry the product topology, with projections $\pi_j$
   42	([[def-product-topology]]). Then:
   43	
   44	1. **The projections are continuous**, and the product topology is the coarsest
   45	   topology on $P$ making all of them continuous.
   46	2. **Characteristic property.** For every space $Z$ and every function
   47	   $h : Z \to P$,
   48	   $$h \text{ is continuous } \iff \pi_i \circ h \text{ is continuous for every } i \in I .$$
   49	   The functions $\pi_i \circ h$ are the **components** of $h$, and every family
   50	   of functions $h_i : Z \to X_i$ arises from exactly one $h$, namely
   51	   $h(z)(i) := h_i(z)$.
   52	3. **The projections are open maps** ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]), for
   53	   the product topology and for the box topology alike. They need not be closed;
   54	   that failure is recorded on this page as a false statement.
   55	4. **Surjectivity.** If every $X_i$ is nonempty then every $\pi_j$ is surjective.
   56	   For $I$ a natural number this is a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]); for
   57	   an arbitrary $I$ it is the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), and this
   58	   is the only place in the item where a choice principle is used.
   59	
   60	## Facts & Assumptions
   61	
   62	**Given:** Topological spaces $(X_i,\mathcal{T}_i)_{i \in I}$, the product $P = \prod_{i \in I} X_i$ with the product topology and the projections $\pi_j(x) = x_j$, a space $Z$ and a function $h : Z \to P$, and an index $j \in I$.
   63	
   64	[A1] The product topology on $P$ is the initial topology of $(\pi_i)_{i \in I}$, and a basis for it is the family of boxes $\prod_i U_i$ with every $U_i$ open and $U_i = X_i$ for all but finitely many $i$; a basis for the box topology is the family of all boxes $\prod_i U_i$ with every $U_i$ open ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
   65	
   66	[A2] $f$ is an open map when $f[U]$ is open in the target for every open $U$ in the source ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
   67	
   68	[L1] For a topology given as an initial topology of a family $(f_i)$: each $f_i$ is continuous, the topology is the coarsest with that property, and a map $h$ into it is continuous exactly when every $f_i \circ h$ is ([[thm-initial-and-final-characteristic-properties]], claims 1 and 2; [[def-initial-and-final-topology]], [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
   69	
   70	[L2] If $F$ is a function with domain a natural number $n$ whose values are nonempty sets, then the family of its values has a choice function ([[lem-finite-choice]], [[def-choice-function]]).
   71	
   72	[L3] If every member of a family of sets is nonempty then the product of the family is nonempty; this is the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-choice-function]]).
   73	
   74	[L4] The image of a union is the union of the images, and an arbitrary union of open sets is open ([[def-topological-space]]).
   75	
   76	## Proof
   77	
   78	**Proof technique:** direct.
   79	
   80	1.1 By [A1] the product topology is an initial topology, so [L1] gives claim 1 and claim 2 at once, the defining family being $(\pi_i)_{i \in I}$. [A1, L1]
   81	
   82	1.2 For a family of functions $h_i : Z \to X_i$ the assignment $h(z)(i) := h_i(z)$ defines a function $Z \to P$, since $h(z)$ has domain $I$ and $h(z)(i) = h_i(z) \in X_i$; it satisfies $\pi_i \circ h = h_i$, and any $h'$ with $\pi_i \circ h' = h_i$ for every $i$ satisfies $h'(z)(i) = h_i(z) = h(z)(i)$ for all $z$ and $i$, hence $h' = h$. [given]
   83	
   84	1.3 Let $B = \prod_i U_i$ be a box with every $U_i$ open. If $B = \varnothing$ then $\pi_j[B] = \varnothing$. If $B \ne \varnothing$, fix $b \in B$; then $\pi_j[B] = U_j$, since $\pi_j[B] \subseteq U_j$ by definition, and for $u \in U_j$ the function $y$ with $y_j := u$ and $y_i := b_i$ for $i \ne j$ lies in $B$ and has $\pi_j(y) = u$. [A1, choose]
   85	
   86	1.4 Assume every $X_i$ is nonempty and $I$ is a natural number $n$. By [L2] applied to $i \mapsto X_i$ there is a choice function $g$ for the family of values, and $x(i) := g(X_i)$ defines a point of $P$; so $P \ne \varnothing$. [L2]
   87	
   88	1.5 Assume every $X_i$ is nonempty and $I$ is arbitrary. By [L3] the product $P$ is nonempty. [L3]
   89	
   90	2.1 Both the box topology and the product topology have a basis consisting of boxes, by [A1], and the image of a union of basic sets is the union of their images; so by step 1.3 the image under $\pi_j$ of any open set of either topology is a union of sets each of which is $\varnothing$ or an open $U_j \subseteq X_j$, hence open. This is claim 3. [step 1.3, A1, A2, L4]
   91	
   92	2.2 Assume every $X_i$ is nonempty and let $t \in X_j$. By step 1.4 when $I$ is a natural number, and by step 1.5 in general, there is a point $p \in P$; the function $y$ with $y_j := t$ and $y_i := p_i$ for $i \ne j$ then lies in $P$ and satisfies $\pi_j(y) = t$. So $\pi_j$ is surjective, which is claim 4. [step 1.4, step 1.5]
   93	
   94	3.1 Step 1.1 gives claims 1 and 2, step 1.2 gives the bijection between maps into $P$ and families of component maps, step 2.1 gives claim 3 and step 2.2 gives claim 4. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2] ∎
   95	
   96	## Remarks
   97	
   98	- **Exactly where choice is spent, and where it is not.** Openness of the
   99	  projections (claim 3) is choice free: step 1.3 uses a *single* point of the box
  100	  in question, which is given by the assumption that the box is nonempty, and
  101	  builds the required preimage from it by changing one coordinate. Surjectivity
  102	  (claim 4) is different, because there the point has to be produced from nothing
  103	  but nonemptiness of the factors, and for an infinite index set that is the
  104	  Axiom of Choice itself.
  105	
  106	- **The characteristic property is what makes the product topology the right
  107	  one.** The box topology has no analogue of claim 2: a map into a box-topologised
  108	  product may have all components continuous and fail to be continuous, and the
  109	  companion page exhibits the diagonal of $\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}$ doing exactly
  110	  that.
  111	
  112	- **Openness does not survive to closedness.** A projection is always open and is
  113	  in general not closed, and the standard witness, the hyperbola in
  114	  $\mathbb{R}^2$, is worked in the false statement on this page. There is no
  115	  asymmetry of taste here: images of open boxes are computed coordinatewise,
  116	  while a closed set of the product need not be a union of closed boxes at all.

