    1	---
    2	id: cor-archimedean-reciprocal
    3	kind: corollary
    4	title: "For every $\\varepsilon > 0$ in a complete ordered field there is a natural $n \\ge 1$ with $1/n < \\varepsilon$"
    5	status: published
    6	origin: session
    7	provenance:
    8	  statement: ai-altered
    9	  proof: ai-altered
   10	deps: [thm-of-archimedean, lem-of-inverse-positive, def-complete-ordered-field, def-ordered-field, def-field]
   11	justified_by: []
   12	aliases: []
   13	landmark: false
   14	proof_strategy: direct
   15	verification:
   16	  precheck: pass
   17	  judge:
   18	    model: z-ai/glm-5.2
   19	    verdict: pass
   20	    date: 2026-07-26
   21	  audited: 2026-07-26
   22	sources:
   23	  scraped: []
   24	  references:
   25	    - title: "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Ch. 1 (Thm 1.20(a) and its corollaries)"
   26	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principles_of_Mathematical_Analysis"
   27	    - title: "T. Tao, Analysis I, 3rd ed., §5.4 (Prop. 5.4.12, the Archimedean property)"
   28	      url: "https://terrytao.wordpress.com/books/analysis-i/"
   29	    - title: "Archimedean property (Wikipedia)"
   30	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archimedean_property"
   31	    - title: "UTSA Mathematics: The Archimedean property"
   32	      url: "https://mathresearch.utsa.edu/wiki/index.php?title=Real_Numbers%3AArchimedean_Property"
   33	---
   34	
   35	## Statement
   36	
   37	Let $F$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and let
   38	$\varepsilon \in F$ with $\varepsilon > 0$. Then there is a natural number
   39	$n \ge 1$ such that
   40	
   41	$$\frac{1}{n \cdot 1_F} < \varepsilon ,$$
   42	
   43	where $n \cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of $F$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]]) and
   44	$1/(n \cdot 1_F)$ is its multiplicative inverse ([[def-field]]). As is standard
   45	we abbreviate $n \cdot 1_F$ to $n$ and write the conclusion $1/n < \varepsilon$.
   46	
   47	This is the **reciprocal form** of the Archimedean property.
   48	[[thm-of-archimedean]] on its own delivers only the assertion that the canonical
   49	naturals are cofinal, $x < n \cdot 1_F$; the form actually used in analysis, that
   50	the reciprocals of the naturals get below every positive bound, is the statement
   51	above, and it is recorded separately so that no proof has to reconstruct the
   52	inversion step in passing.
   53	
   54	## Facts & Assumptions
   55	
   56	**Given:** A complete ordered field $F$ and an element $\varepsilon \in F$ with $\varepsilon > 0$.
   57	
   58	[L1] Archimedean property: for every $x \in F$ there is a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $x < n \cdot 1_F$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
   59	
   60	[L2] Inverses and order: if $a > 0$ then $a^{-1} > 0$, and if $0 < a < b$ then $0 < b^{-1} < a^{-1}$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
   61	
   62	[L3] Field arithmetic: an element $a$ with $a > 0$ is nonzero by trichotomy, hence has a multiplicative inverse $a^{-1}$, and $(a^{-1})^{-1} = a$ ([[def-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
   63	
   64	## Proof
   65	
   66	**Proof technique:** direct.
   67	
   68	1.1 Since $\varepsilon > 0$, trichotomy gives $\varepsilon \ne 0$, so $\varepsilon^{-1}$ exists, and $\varepsilon^{-1} > 0$. [given, L2, L3]
   69	
   70	2.1 Apply [L1] to $x = \varepsilon^{-1}$: fix a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $\varepsilon^{-1} < n \cdot 1_F$. [step 1.1, L1, choose]
   71	
   72	3.1 Chaining the two displayed inequalities gives $0 < \varepsilon^{-1} < n \cdot 1_F$; in particular $n \cdot 1_F > 0$, so $n \cdot 1_F \ne 0$ and $1/(n \cdot 1_F)$ is defined. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L3]
   73	
   74	4.1 Apply the second claim of [L2] with $a = \varepsilon^{-1}$ and $b = n \cdot 1_F$: $0 < (n \cdot 1_F)^{-1} < (\varepsilon^{-1})^{-1}$. [step 3.1, L2]
   75	
   76	5.1 By [L3], $(\varepsilon^{-1})^{-1} = \varepsilon$, so the natural number $n \ge 1$ fixed in step 2.1 satisfies $1/(n \cdot 1_F) < \varepsilon$, which is the assertion. [step 4.1, L3] ∎
   77	
   78	## Remarks
   79	
   80	- **Monotonicity gives the eventual form for free.** If $m \ge n \ge 1$ then
   81	  $m \cdot 1_F \ge n \cdot 1_F > 0$, because $k \mapsto k \cdot 1_F$ is strictly
   82	  increasing on the naturals $\ge 1$ ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and so
   83	  $1/(m \cdot 1_F) \le 1/(n \cdot 1_F) < \varepsilon$ by [[lem-of-inverse-positive]]
   84	  again. So the corollary yields not merely one index but a threshold: every
   85	  $m \ge n$ satisfies $1/m < \varepsilon$. That one extra line is what a
   86	  convergence proof needs, and it is left to the caller rather than folded into
   87	  the statement, because the caller usually has a threshold of its own to combine
   88	  it with.
   89	
   90	- **Completeness is used only through [[thm-of-archimedean]].** Nothing here
   91	  needs the least-upper-bound property directly. The corollary therefore holds
   92	  verbatim in any Archimedean ordered field, in particular in $\mathbb{Q}$, and
   93	  it fails in a non-Archimedean ordered field, where an infinitesimal
   94	  $\varepsilon > 0$ is below every $1/n$ by construction.
   95	
   96	- The equivalence is exact: the reciprocal form implies the cofinal form back
   97	  again, since given $x > 0$ one applies it to $\varepsilon = 1/x$. The two are
   98	  the same property written on the two sides of the inversion, and only the
   99	  direction proved above is used in this library.
