   16	verification:
   17	  precheck: n/a
   18	  audited: 2026-07-27
   19	sources:
   20	  scraped: []
   21	  references:
   22	    - title: "Characteristic (algebra) (Wikipedia)"
   23	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Characteristic_(algebra)"
   24	    - title: "T. Tao, Analysis I, 3rd ed."
   25	      url: "https://terrytao.wordpress.com/books/analysis-i/"
   26	    - title: "Elias Zakon, Mathematical Analysis: Natural Numbers and Induction"
   27	      url: "https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analysis/Mathematical_Analysis_%28Zakon%29/02%3A_Real_Numbers_and_Fields/2.02%3A_Natural_Numbers._Induction"
   28	pipeline_run: null
   29	---
   30	
   31	## Definition
   32	
   33	Let $F$ be a field ([[def-field]]) with additive identity $0_F$ and
   34	multiplicative identity $1_F$. Define
   35	$\iota : \mathbb{N} \to F$ by recursion on $\mathbb{N}$
   36	([[def-natural-numbers]], [[thm-recursion]]):
   37	
   38	$$\iota(0) \;:=\; 0_F, \qquad \iota(n+1) \;:=\; \iota(n) + 1_F .$$
   39	
   40	$\iota(n)$ is the **canonical natural** of $n$ in $F$. It is also written
   41	$n \cdot 1_F$, and for $n \ge 1$ it is $1_F$ added to itself $n$ times.
   42	
   43	**Why the notation is needed at all.** A natural number in this library is a von
   44	Neumann natural, that is a set ([[def-natural-numbers]]), and a set is not an
   45	element of $F$. So $1/n$, $n^{\alpha}$ and $x/n$ are not expressions of $F$ when
   46	$n$ is a natural: what they mean is $1/\iota(n)$, $\iota(n)^{\alpha}$ and
   47	$x/\iota(n)$. The map $\iota$ is what carries a counting number into the field,
   48	and writing it is the whole reason a reader meets $1/\iota(k+1)$ where an
   49	informal text would write $1/(k+1)$.
   50	
   51	## Remarks
   52	
   53	- **Where the index shift comes from.** $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$
   54	  ([[def-natural-numbers]]) and $\iota(0) = 0_F$, so $1/\iota(k)$ is undefined at
   55	  $k = 0$. A family of reciprocals indexed by $\mathbb{N}$ is therefore written
   56	  over $\iota(k+1)$, which is why the harmonic and telescoping families of this
   57	  library run over $k+1$ rather than over $k$. This is bookkeeping, not a
   58	  restriction: the values are the usual ones.
   59	
   60	- **This definition records notation; the arithmetic is proved elsewhere.** That
   61	  $\iota$ is strictly increasing and positive on $n \ge 1$, and that it carries
   62	  sums to sums and products to products, is [[lem-of-naturals-positive]], stated
   63	  for an ordered field. That lemma introduces the same element by the equivalent
   64	  recursion $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F$, $(n+1) \cdot 1_F = n \cdot 1_F + 1_F$, which
   65	  agrees with the definition above because $\iota(1) = \iota(0) + 1_F = 1_F$.
   66	  Nothing here is new mathematics; the definition exists so that the notation has
   67	  a home a reader can look up.
   68	
   69	- **The symbol $\iota$ is used in this library for other canonical maps, and this
   70	  definition does not govern them.** It also denotes the canonical field
   71	  embedding $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]), the isometric
   72	  embedding of a metric space into a completion ([[def-metric-completion]]), and
   73	  an inclusion map $\iota : S \to X$ of a subspace. Each of those is introduced
   74	  where it is used and means something different from the map defined here. What
   75	  the four share is only that each is the canonical map of its situation.
   76	
   77	- **Fields, not just ordered fields.** The recursion needs no order, so the
   78	  definition is stated for a field; every use in this library is in an ordered
   79	  field, and the order is what makes $\iota$ injective
   80	  ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]). In a field of positive characteristic $\iota$
   81	  is not injective, which is one reason the injectivity is a lemma rather than
   82	  part of the definition.
